.TH KILLALL 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
killall \- send a signal to all processes with a given name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B killall
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-\c
.IR signal ]
.IR name ...
.br
.B killall
.RB [ \-q ]
.B \-s
.I signal
.IR name ...
.br
.B killall
.RB [ \-q ]
.B \-\-
.IR name ...
.br
.B killall \-l
.RI [ signal ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B killall
sends a signal, by default
.BR TERM ,
to every running process whose name is one of the
.I name
operands.
It takes the operands in order, and reports each one that matches no
process.
It never signals its own process, but a
.I name
matched only by that process, such as
.BR killall ,
still counts as matched.
It reads no input.
With
.BR \-l ,
it instead converts signal names to numbers and back, writing the
results on standard output.
.PP
Messages go to standard error.
A
.I name
that matches no process is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
killall: \fIname\fR: no process killed
.EE
.RE
.PP
and a process that could not be signaled as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
killall: can\(aqt kill pid \fIpid\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I reason
is usually
.B Permission denied
or
.BR "Invalid argument" .
.PP
This
.B killall
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
A
.I name
is an exact process name, not a pattern.
To select processes by pattern, use
.BR pkill (1).
For what the signals do on Windows, see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Process names
Only the name of a process is matched, never its path or command line.
The name is the file name of its executable as Windows reports it,
including the extension, such as
.BR explorer.exe .
The comparison is case-sensitive, although Windows file names are not,
and the extension is part of the name, so
.B server
does not match
.BR server.exe .
.PP
A process running the same build of busybox is named after its applet
instead, such as
.B sh
or
.BR sleep ,
unless
.B killall
cannot read its memory, as for an elevated process, which leaves it
.BR busybox.exe .
A script whose
.B #!\&
line names an applet is named after the script file, except that a shell
script run from the kit's shell is named after the shell, such as
.BR sh .
The kit's command launchers, such as
.B sleep.exe
and
.BR killall.exe ,
appear under their own names, each alongside the busybox process it
started, so
.B killall sleep
signals the busybox process and leaves the launcher to exit with it.
.PP
Only the first 15 characters of a long name are compared.
A process name of 15 or more characters matches any operand that agrees
with it in its first 15 characters, however the two differ after that,
so the extension may be left off a name with 15 or more characters
before it.
A shorter process name must match the whole operand.
.PP
The idle process, with process ID 0 and the name
.BR "[System\ Process]" ,
is never selected.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognized only at the start of the command line.
First comes
.BR \-q ,
if given, and then one of
.BR \-s ,
.BI \- signal\fR,
or
.BR \-\- .
.B \-l
and
.B \-\-help
are recognized only as the first argument.
Every later argument is a
.IR name ,
even one that begins with a dash, and options cannot be grouped.
.TP
.B \-l
List or convert signals, then exit without signaling anything.
With no further arguments, write one signal per line as a number, a
parenthesis, a space, and a name, such as
.B "\ 9)\ KILL"
and
.BR "15)\ TERM" .
The list is
.BR HUP ,
.BR INT ,
.BR QUIT ,
.BR ILL ,
.BR FPE ,
.BR KILL ,
.BR SEGV ,
.BR PIPE ,
.BR TERM ,
and
.BR ABRT ,
numbered 1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 11, 13, 15, and 22.
.IP
With arguments, write a line for each, converting them in order.
A name, in any case and with or without a
.B SIG
prefix, is converted to its number.
A decimal number is reduced to its low 7 bits, so that a shell exit
status of 128 plus a signal number gives that signal's name, and is
written as that name, or as the reduced number when it has none.
An argument that is neither stops the conversion with
.B unknown signal
and exit status 1, after the lines for earlier arguments.
.TP
.B \-q
Do not report names that match no process, or processes that could not
be signaled.
Each still counts toward the exit status.
.TP
.BI "\-s " signal
Send
.I signal
instead of
.BR TERM .
The signal may also be attached, as in
.BR \-sTERM .
It is otherwise the same as
.BI \- signal
below, and the usage message does not list it.
.TP
.BI \- signal
Send
.I signal
instead of
.BR TERM .
It is a name, in any case and with or without a
.B SIG
prefix, or a decimal number from 0 to 22.
The names are those listed by
.BR \-l ,
plus
.B EXIT
for 0.
Any other name or number is rejected with
.B bad signal name
and exit status 1, before any process is looked at.
An argument that begins with a lowercase
.B s
is taken as
.B \-s
with the signal attached, so
.B \-sigterm
and
.B \-segv
are bad signal names; write
.B \-SIGTERM
or
.BR \-SEGV .
.IP
Signal 0 sends nothing, but still fails for a process that could not be
signaled, so it tests whether each match exists and can be signaled.
A number from 0 to 22 with no name, such as 21, is accepted but fails
with
.B Invalid argument
for each process it is sent to.
So does an empty name, or
.B SIG
alone, which is taken as 5.
.TP
.B \-\-
Ends the options, so that the first
.I name
may begin with a dash.
It is recognized only in place of a signal.
After
.BI \- signal
or
.BI "\-s " signal ,
it is a name.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded as file names before
.B killall
sees them, even inside quotes, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A name is never a pattern, so the effect is only that an operand such as
.B app*
is replaced by the names of matching files in the current directory,
which are then taken as process names.
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
A busybox process started with the variable unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
The startup code shared by all applets also reads
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
neither of which changes what
.B killall
does, and variables such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I name
matched a process and each match was signaled, or
.B \-l
converted every argument, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line: a bad signal name, no
.IR name ,
a lone
.BR \-q ,
which also prints the usage message on standard error, or an unknown
signal given to
.BR \-l .
.TP
.I n
The number of failures, with or without
.BR \-q :
one for each
.I name
that matches no process, and one for each process that could not be
signaled.
A single failure also gives 1.
Every operand is tried, whatever fails.
The status is this count modulo 256, so 256 failures give 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Terminate every instance of a program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ killall server.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the report and the exit status when nothing matches:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ killall server.exe; echo $?
killall: server.exe: no process killed
1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Stay silent when the program is not running:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ killall \-q server.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
End a program at once, without letting it clean up:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ killall \-KILL server.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test, without signaling it, whether a program is running and could be
signaled:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ killall \-q \-0 server.exe && echo running
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert between signal numbers and names, such as the status 143 that
the shell reports for a process ended by
.BR TERM :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ killall \-l 143
TERM
$ killall \-l kill
9
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the psmisc
.BR killall ,
and from busybox on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
Only a process's name is matched.
Busybox on Linux also matches the name a program was started with, and
the name or full path of its executable.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no options to select processes by pattern, owner, or age, or
to wait for them to exit.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the ten signals listed by
.B \-l
exist.
There is no
.BR USR1 ,
.BR ALRM ,
.BR STOP ,
or
.BR CONT .
.IP \(bu 2
Names include the extension and are case-sensitive.
.IP \(bu 2
Failures to signal a process are reported and counted.
.BR pkill (1)
ignores them.
.PP
An operand is a name, never a path, so one such as
.I ./server.exe
or
.I C:/bin/server.exe
matches no process.
The list of processes is read again for each operand, and covers every
process on the system, including those of other users.
.PP
Windows cannot send signals between processes, so
.B killall
emulates them.
.B KILL
calls
.BR TerminateProcess ,
which gives the process no chance to clean up.
Any other signal except 0 makes the process call
.B ExitProcess
from a thread injected into it, so that its DLLs are notified, but no
signal handler runs and the signal cannot be caught or ignored.
Either way the process ends at once, with an exit code of the signal
number shifted left 24 bits, such as
.B 0x0F000000
for
.BR TERM .
The busybox shell reports that as death by the signal, with status 128
plus the signal number.
Only the matched process is signaled, not its children.
A thread that cannot be created is not reported, so a successful exit
status does not prove that the process exited.
.PP
Injecting a thread works only into a process of the same bitness as
busybox, 64-bit or 32-bit, so for any other process every signal except
.BR KILL ,
including 0, fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
A process that Windows does not let busybox open, such as a system
process or one of another user, fails the same way.
.PP
Only the process running
.B killall
is excluded.
In the kit's shell that is the shell itself, because the shell runs
.B killall
without starting a process, so
.B killall sh
spares the shell that runs it but signals every other busybox shell.
A subshell or a pipeline element is a separate process, so there the
shell it was started from is signaled too.
Started from
.B cmd.exe
through the launcher
.BR killall.exe ,
the launcher is a separate process and is not excluded.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR pgrep (1),
.BR pidof (1),
.BR pkill (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR sh (1)
